{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 23, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by political instability as his father was assassinated when he was only twelve years old. This led to a long regency during which the County of Tripoli was governed by nobles and relatives until Raymond came of age. His minority was a turbulent time as the region faced threats from Muslim forces and internal power struggles among the Crusader states. Despite his youth Raymond was eventually recognized as the legitimate ruler and took full control of his county.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III was known for his cautious and diplomatic approach to governance. He often sought alliances through marriage and negotiation rather than warfare. He married twice first to a daughter of the Byzantine Emperor and later to a noblewoman from the Kingdom of Jerusalem. These marriages strengthened his ties with neighboring Christian states. He was also a close advisor to King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was named regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during Baldwin’s illness and played a critical role in managing military and political affairs. His leadership during this time was controversial as some nobles accused him of being too passive and hesitant to confront Saladin.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond’s relationship with Saladin was complex. He sometimes negotiated truces with the Muslim leader and was even suspected of secret dealings. These suspicions led to his temporary exile from Jerusalem after a failed plot against him. He was later restored to favor but his influence continued to wane. Raymond was present at the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Crusader army was crushed by Saladin. He survived the battle and fled to Tripoli but died shortly after in October 1187. His death marked the end of an era as the County of Tripoli struggled to maintain its independence in the wake of the Crusader collapse. Raymond III is remembered as a cautious nobleman who tried to preserve peace in a time of relentless conflict. His legacy is one of diplomacy amid chaos and a life spent navigating the treacherous politics of the Levant.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.47409361600875854}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0026736250147223473}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.2993727978027891e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -5.